    {
      "id": "jYyrDSnwQmHvt-bGpUaRo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 7665.676550956465,
      "y": -2366.55991011486,
      "width": 1345.846923828125,
      "height": 1470,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "wPAAU9N9EdHvrb_iyZiUT"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 456662519,
      "version": 2297,
      "versionNonce": 1587725015,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "I31mbda1NKYWCxtYWMUJd",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1784877911686,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Proposed architecture : \n\n1. ALB with Waf + EC2 / ECS \n\nPros: \n    - ALB with EC2/ECS target group does not impose any limit on request body\n    -  Request header size is imposed in KBs ( which is not concern for current requirement) \n    - With ECS only integration handles auto spawn of tasks / easy scalable ( which is not \n    our concern as we might encounter 10 calls per minute of 10 MB payloads )\n    - A single EC2 can also be enough for our use case \nCons: \n    - AWS ALB with Waf support 8KB of request body inspection\n\nRemarks : \n    - We can get away with full body inspection with stricter schema validation\n    -  Put rate limiting \n    -  strictly block payload greater than 20MB \n    -  keep AWS Waf for general OWASP filterations.\n\n2. ALB + ECS ( WAF side car + application )\n\nPros:\n    - Use open source wafs to do full body inspection ( greater than 8KB)\nCons:\n    - Full body inspection is not free , will introduce latency ( need a POC to  check how much time is spent )\n    - Open source WAF needs to be maintained \n \n\n\n                                           Waf sidecar                  App\n\n    \n                                                      \n                                                  ECS Task\nApp responsibility: \n - Authorization check\n - Schema validation ( parser depth security check , etc.. ) \n\nWaf side car\n- Full body inspection \n- rate limiting \n- OWASP filtrations",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Proposed architecture : \n\n1. ALB with Waf + EC2 / ECS \n\nPros: \n    - ALB with EC2/ECS target group does not impose any limit on request body\n    -  Request header size is imposed in KBs ( which is not concern for current requirement) \n    - With ECS only integration handles auto spawn of tasks / easy scalable ( which is not \n    our concern as we might encounter 10 calls per minute of 10 MB payloads )\n    - A single EC2 can also be enough for our use case \nCons: \n    - AWS ALB with Waf support 8KB of request body inspection\n\nRemarks : \n    - We can get away with full body inspection with stricter schema validation\n    -  Put rate limiting \n    -  strictly block payload greater than 20MB \n    -  keep AWS Waf for general OWASP filterations.\n\n2. ALB + ECS ( WAF side car + application )\n\nPros:\n    - Use open source wafs to do full body inspection ( greater than 8KB)\nCons:\n    - Full body inspection is not free , will introduce latency ( need a POC to  check how much time is spent )\n    - Open source WAF needs to be maintained \n \n\n\n                                           Waf sidecar                  App\n\n    \n                                                      \n                                                  ECS Task\nApp responsibility: \n - Authorization check\n - Schema validation ( parser depth security check , etc.. ) \n\nWaf side car\n- Full body inspection \n- rate limiting \n- OWASP filtrations",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
